sweet ufs: read straight into the caller's buffer, no bounce copy

bp17/bp18 stop with the UFS lock held, no command outstanding, and "ss 3"
for a minute: the strategy path sits between a 272 KiB read and the copy
out of the bounce buffer.  The data is already in the buffer when the read
returns, so the copy is pure overhead in the common case: file I/O is
block aligned and the buffer is page aligned.

Program the PRDT with one entry per page of the destination and let the
engine write DRAM directly, with cache maintenance by physical address.
EINVAL (a page not mapped) falls back to the bounce path, and partial
blocks still read-merge-write through it.  Heartbeat gains dma <n>/<bytes>
so a boot proves which path served the dyld cache read.
